														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1869489">
									

<p>Yeah, that's not quite what I meant when I suggested parrots. What i meant is, if you teach a parrot to say &quot;Hello&quot;, and then it says &quot;Hello&quot; within earshot of the affected subject, the test subject will then be able to perceive the word &quot;Hello&quot; (as long as you say it like the parrot did, &quot;hellOOOOOO&quot; &#8212; it's like the difference between the word &quot;chirp&quot; and an actual chirp).</p>
<p>Teach the parrot a few simple phrases - &quot;come here&quot;, &quot;eat this&quot;, &quot;good girl&quot;. <em>If</em> the parrot can then be coaxed into speaking those phrases in front of the subject; from that point on, the subject's brain will consider those to be &quot;bird noises&quot; &#8212; and thus, when the subject hears those phrases, they will not be anomalously replaced with bird noises.</p>
<p>Of course, since <em>all</em> non-bird noises are being replaced with bird noises, that might reduce the extent to which this method is useful for communication&#8230;</p>
<p>(unless we bring in <a href="/scp-1849">SCP-1849</a>, of course)</p>

